<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>net6.0;net7.0;net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<!-- Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions 10.0.11 declares net6.0/net7.0
		     as "not tested" (both are EOL runtimes) but its assembly is netstandard2.0-compatible
		     and functions correctly there: verified via an actual runtime smoke test (DI container
		     built, AddAutoMapperLite resolved, a real Map<T> call executed and asserted) on net6.0,
		     not just assumed. Suppressed as a known-benign policy warning, not a real break. -->
		<SuppressTfmSupportBuildWarnings>true</SuppressTfmSupportBuildWarnings>

		<!-- NuGet Metadata -->
		<PackageId>AutoMapperLite</PackageId>
		<Version>4.3.0</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>
			AutoMapperLite 4.3.0 — current official release; all prior versions are deprecated.

			• Performance/allocation fix: the compiled mapping delegate lookup
			  (MapBuilder.GetCompiledMap, used by every dispatch path) previously wrote
			  `LazyInitializer.EnsureInitialized(ref _compiledMap, () =&gt; MappingPlanCompiler.Compile(this, config))`
			  directly in the hot method body. That lambda captures `this` and `config`, so C#
			  allocates a new closure object and a new delegate wrapping it on *every single call*
			  to that method, even once the plan is already compiled and the factory is never
			  invoked — the JIT cannot see that the argument goes unused most of the time. This was
			  the single largest source of AutoMapperLite's excess allocation versus manual mapping
			  and Mapster in every scenario with a same-name nested property or a collection: a
			  100-item collection dropped from 13.44 KB to 4.06 KB (now within 3% of Manual's/
			  Mapster's 3.96 KB, was 3.4x worse); a 5,000-item collection dropped from 664.25 KB to
			  195.48 KB (now within 0.06% of Manual's/Mapster's 195.37 KB, was 3.4x worse); Simple/
			  Medium/Nested/DeepNested object mapping now allocate exactly what Manual mapping does
			  (32 B/80 B/80 B/248 B), down from 128 B/176 B/272 B/632 B. Fixed by checking the
			  field directly (Volatile.Read) first and only falling through to the
			  lambda-allocating slow path once, ever, per builder.
			• Performance: IMapper.Map&lt;TSource,TDestination&gt;(TSource) (added in 4.2.0) is now
			  actually faster than the untyped Map&lt;TDestination&gt;(object) overload it was always
			  meant to beat — measurement during this release showed it had regressed *slower*
			  in practice, because each call still round-tripped through
			  IMapperConfig.GetMap&lt;,&gt;() (a plain dictionary lookup, uncached at the Mapper level)
			  plus the above per-call closure allocation, while the untyped overload's cached
			  closure captured an already-resolved delegate directly (no volatile read, no
			  re-lookup). A dedicated per-Mapper-instance cache (mirroring the untyped path's own)
			  now gives the typed overload the same shape: one dictionary lookup keyed by
			  compile-time Type, then a direct delegate invocation, with zero return-value boxing.
			• Verified collection mapping now scales linearly with no per-item overhead creeping
			  back in as size grows: at 100,000 items AutoMapperLite is within 11% of Manual
			  mapping's time and allocates within 0.007% of it (was previously ~3.4x the
			  allocation at any size). Full scenario-by-scenario numbers and methodology, including
			  this release's benchmark methodology caveats, are in docs/performance.html.
			• No public API or behavior changes since 4.2.0 — this release is dispatch-layer
			  allocation/perf work only, verified against the full existing test suite (120 tests)
			  plus a new regression test for self-referential type mapping
			  (Map_HandlesSelfReferentialType_WithAcyclicData) added to guard against a fix that
			  was considered and deliberately NOT made (see CLAUDE.md) because it would have
			  StackOverflowed on self-referential types like `Employee.Manager: Employee` even with
			  fully acyclic data.

			Previous (4.2.0): dispatch-layer performance redesign; all prior versions deprecated.

			• Performance: redesigned the runtime dispatch layer that sits on top of the compiled
			  expression-tree plans introduced in 4.1.0. The 4.1.0 redesign compiled each mapping
			  itself but still resolved *which* compiled delegate to call via a global cache that
			  re-looked-up the destination config on every single call (two dictionary lookups per
			  Map), used runtime GetType()+dictionary-lookup dispatch for every collection item, and
			  routed nested same-name auto-mapped properties through that same per-call dispatch
			  despite the nested type pair being fully known at plan-compile time. 4.2.0 fixes all
			  three: entry-point resolution is now cached per Mapper instance and resolved once, not
			  re-looked-up per call; List&lt;T&gt; mapping resolves the item mapper once per call (not
			  once per item) via a strongly-typed loop with zero per-item boxing; and nested
			  auto-mapped properties (single object and List&lt;T&gt;) have their builder resolved once
			  at plan-compile time and baked directly into the compiled expression. Measured on this
			  repo's own BenchmarkDotNet suite: simple-object mapping 67.6 ns → 42.2 ns (~38%
			  faster, now faster than AutoMapper's 58.2 ns), a 100-item collection 6,986.7 ns →
			  2,744.1 ns (~61% faster), a 5,000-item collection 390.81 µs → 156.47 µs (~60% faster).
			  Cold start remains the fastest of the three compared libraries. Full scenario-by-
			  scenario numbers and methodology are in docs/performance.html.
			• Added: IMapper.Map&lt;TSource, TDestination&gt;(TSource source) — a strongly-typed
			  overload for call sites where the source type is known at compile time. Skips
			  source.GetType(), the entry-point cache, and return-value boxing entirely. This is an
			  addition to the IMapper interface: additive for callers, technically breaking for
			  anyone who implements IMapper themselves rather than using the built-in Mapper class.
			• Behavior change (disclosed): List&lt;T&gt; destination mapping now resolves the item map
			  from the source collection's declared item type (e.g. List&lt;TSourceItem&gt;'s generic
			  argument), not from each element's individual runtime type. A List&lt;Base&gt; containing
			  Derived instances now maps every element through Base's registered map, using Base's
			  properties only — previously this dispatched per element by runtime type, which would
			  throw for a Derived element unless a map was separately registered for exactly
			  (Derived, TDestItem). Source collections whose item type can't be determined from
			  their own Type (non-generic IEnumerable) are unaffected and still dispatch per element
			  by runtime type.
			• No public API breaking changes for callers since 3.0.3 (only for custom IMapper
			  implementers, per the addition above).

			Previous (4.1.0): compiled expression-tree mapping plans replaced interpreted
			reflection (PropertyInfo.GetValue/SetValue, MethodInfo.Invoke, Activator.CreateInstance)
			for the per-property copying work itself; exceptions stopped arriving wrapped in
			TargetInvocationException as a result.

			Previous (4.0.2):
			• Fixed: updated test tooling (xunit.v3, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 18.9.0) hard-required
			  net8.0+ and a Microsoft Testing Platform v2 change dropped dotnet test's legacy
			  VSTest bridge on the .NET 10 SDK — the test project now targets net8.0-net10.0 (the
			  library itself is unaffected and still targets net6.0-net10.0) and a repo-root
			  global.json opts dotnet test into native MTP mode
			• Fixed: Activator.CreateInstance failures (e.g. mapping to a positional record with
			  no parameterless constructor) now throw a clear InvalidOperationException naming
			  the type, instead of a bare MissingMethodException
			• Added real, measured BenchmarkDotNet comparisons against manual mapping, AutoMapper
			  16.2.0, and Mapster 10.0.12 — published in docs/performance.html
			• Verified and documented several previously-untested behaviors: null source
			  properties are skipped (not assigned), auto-map requires an exact type match
			  (different enum types / numeric widening are not converted), and records with a
			  parameterless constructor + init-only properties map correctly
		</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<Authors>Atik Hassan</Authors>
		<Company>AutoMapperLite</Company>
		<Description>AutoMapperLite is a lightweight object-to-object mapper for .NET — a small, dependency-minimal alternative to AutoMapper for mapping DTOs and entities. Each source/destination type pair is compiled into a cached delegate via System.Linq.Expressions on first use, rather than interpreted via reflection on every call. Targets net6.0 through net10.0. Features fluent CreateMap/ForMember/ForPath profiles, nested object and List&lt;T&gt; collection mapping, thread-safe configuration, and singleton-friendly dependency injection via AddAutoMapperLite. Built for projects that want a simple, dependency-minimal mapper without a large footprint; see the published benchmarks for exactly how it compares to AutoMapper and Mapster. Official site: https://automapperlite.atkhssn.info</Description>
		<PackageTags>mapper;object mapper;object mapping;dto mapper;dto mapping;automapper alternative;mapster alternative;dotnet mapper;csharp mapper;lightweight mapper;expression mapper</PackageTags>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://automapperlite.atkhssn.info</PackageProjectUrl>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/atkhssn/AutoMapperLite</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<Copyright>© 2025-2026 Atik Hassan</Copyright>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- Files to include in NuGet -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="AutoMapperLite.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<Compile Remove="AutoMapperLite.Tests\**" />
		<Compile Remove="AutoMapperLite.Benchmarks\**" />
		<EmbeddedResource Remove="AutoMapperLite.Tests\**" />
		<EmbeddedResource Remove="AutoMapperLite.Benchmarks\**" />
		<None Remove="AutoMapperLite.Tests\**" />
		<None Remove="AutoMapperLite.Benchmarks\**" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>